            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Data Annotations Validation</h2>
                    <p>Data annotations provide a simple, declarative way to validate incoming DTOs at the model level. Use attributes like <code>[Required]</code>, <code>[StringLength]</code>, and <code>[Range]</code> to enforce rules that run during model binding on the server.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public class CreateProductDto
{
    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Name is required")]
    [StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 2)]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [StringLength(500)]
    public string? Description { get; set; }

    [Required]
    [Range(0.01, 999999.99, ErrorMessage = "Price must be between 0.01 and 999999.99")]
    public decimal Price { get; set; }

    [Required]
    [Range(0, int.MaxValue)]
    public int Stock { get; set; }

    [EmailAddress]
    public string? ContactEmail { get; set; }

    [Url]
    public string? Website { get; set; }

    [RegularExpression("^[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{4}$", ErrorMessage = "Invalid SKU format")]
    public string? Sku { get; set; }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Automatic Validation with [ApiController]</h2>
                    <p>Applying <code>[ApiController]</code> to a controller enables automatic model validation: if a bound model fails validation, ASP.NET Core short-circuits and returns a 400 with a Problem Details payload. This lets your action methods assume the model is valid and keep handler code focused on business logic.</p>
                    <p>If you need custom behavior (for example, a different error shape or logging), override the default response by configuring <code>InvalidModelStateResponseFactory</code> or write a custom filter.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">[HttpPost]
public ActionResult&lt;Product&gt; Create(CreateProductDto dto)
{
    // If validation fails, returns 400 automatically
    // You only reach here if model is valid
    
    var product = MapToProduct(dto);
    _repository.Add(product);
    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(GetById), new { id = product.Id }, product);
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Custom Validation Attributes</h2>
                    <p>For validation logic that doesn't fit built-in attributes, create a custom <code>ValidationAttribute</code>. This keeps validation close to the model and is evaluated during model binding like other data annotations.</p>
                    <p>Keep custom attributes simple and side-effect free; for complex or stateful validation prefer a separate validator class so logic stays testable.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public class FutureDateAttribute : ValidationAttribute
{
    protected override ValidationResult? IsValid(object? value, ValidationContext context)
    {
        if (value is DateTime date &amp;&amp; date &lt;= DateTime.Now)
        {
            return new ValidationResult("Date must be in the future");
        }
        return ValidationResult.Success;
    }
}

// Usage
public class CreateEventDto
{
    [Required]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [FutureDate]
    public DateTime EventDate { get; set; }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>FluentValidation</h2>
                    <p>FluentValidation is a popular external library that provides a fluent API for expressive, testable validation rules. It is useful when validation requires complex conditional logic or multiple related rules.</p>
                    <p>Register validator types with the DI container so they integrate with the ASP.NET Core pipeline and produce model validation errors automatically.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet add package FluentValidation.AspNetCore</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public class CreateProductDtoValidator : AbstractValidator&lt;CreateProductDto&gt;
{
    public CreateProductDtoValidator()
    {
        RuleFor(x => x.Name)
            .NotEmpty().WithMessage("Name is required")
            .Length(2, 100);

        RuleFor(x => x.Price)
            .GreaterThan(0).WithMessage("Price must be positive")
            .LessThanOrEqualTo(999999.99m);

        RuleFor(x => x.Stock)
            .GreaterThanOrEqualTo(0);

        RuleFor(x => x.ContactEmail)
            .EmailAddress()
            .When(x => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(x.ContactEmail));
    }
}

// Register in Program.cs
builder.Services.AddValidatorsFromAssemblyContaining&lt;CreateProductDtoValidator&gt;();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Problem Details Response</h2>
                    <p>When validation fails the framework returns a Problem Details response (RFC 7807) that includes a top-level error title, status, and an <code>errors</code> map listing each invalid field and its messages. This standardized shape makes API errors easier for clients to parse.</p>
                    <p>You can customize the contents or mapping of validation errors by providing a custom <code>ProblemDetailsFactory</code> or by transforming the model state in a filter or middleware.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7231#section-6.5.1",
  "title": "One or more validation errors occurred.",
  "status": 400,
  "errors": {
    "Name": ["Name is required"],
    "Price": ["Price must be between 0.01 and 999999.99"]
  }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Data Annotations for simple validation rules.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>[ApiController]</code> automatically returns 400 for invalid models.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use FluentValidation for complex validation scenarios.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Problem Details provides a standardized error format.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
